Defects, a kind of disruption to the periodic atomic arrangements in lattice, are almost universal in the structure of solid materials, despite the great efforts made to achieve minimum system energy.[1] The formation of structural defects is generally accompanied by bond breaking and re-forming, lattice distortion, electron localization, etc., which would induce property changes in various applications. For instance, short-range disordered structural defects can behave as scattering centers by breaking electronic structure and cause localized band-tail states, thus affecting the mobility and conductivity.[1] Therefore, defects have long been regarded as unfavorable imperfections that can deteriorate the performance of materials.[2] On the contrary, indepth experimental and theoretical studies recently revealed the importance of defects in tailoring the local properties of solid materials and endowing them with new functionalities and/or enhanced performances.[3] Particularly, the control of lattice defects in semiconductive materials may be able to modulate the electronic and band structures, carrier concentration, and even the conductivity.[3] Moreover, from the perspective of surface science and catalysis, the coordinatively unsaturated sites associated with surface defects can serve as active sites for molecular absorption and activation.[4] Therefore, the rational creation of structural defects may offer the opportunity to tune and optimize the performance. Given the subtle influence of defects on properties, especially catalysis, it is of great importance to employ a suitable model and rationally engineer structural defects for a deeper understanding of the relationship between defect control and performance.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of crystalline porous material, are assembled by inorganic metal ions/clusters with organic linkers.[5] The periodic and well-defined structures of MOFs make them ideal candidates for defect studies. Starting from perfect single-crystal structures, the rational introduction of structural defects into MOFs is feasible by regulating the synthetic parameters that may affect the properties. Given the unique advantages of MOFs, including high surface area, wellexposed active sites, free transport of substrates/products in the pores, etc., there have been intensive explorations toward catalysis and photocatalysis based on structural modulation and decoration in recent years.[6] Although a few recent investigations on MOF defects have suggested the preliminary influences on catalysis and sorption,[7] to the best of our knowledge, there has never been experimental exploration to clarify how different levels of structural defects in MOFs systematically affect the photocatalytic performance.
The representative MOF, UiO-66-NH2,[8] is a promising choice for this study due to its facile synthesis, great stability, structural tailorability, and high tolerance for structural modulation.[7] Previous theoretical have studies indicated that the removal of linkers in UiO-66 lowers the unoccupied d orbitals of Zr atoms, which may increase the likelihood of charge transfer in the photocatalytic process.[9] In this context, with controlled defect engineering, UiO-66-NH2 may be an ideal platform to systematically investigate the relationship between structural defects (level) and photocatalytic performance.
Bearing these considerations in mind, we have rationally synthesized UiO-66-NH2 with different contents of structural defects, denoted as $\mathsf{J i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}-X}$ ( where ${\sf X}=0$ , 50, 100, 150, 200, representing the molar equivalents of the modulator with respect to the linker upon synthesis), for photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production, in the presence of $\mathsf{P t}$ nanoparticles (NPs) as cocatalyst (Scheme 1). Along with the increase in structural defects, the photocatalytic $H_{2}$ production rate gradually increases. However, unlike previous reports,[6f,9a] the photocatalytic activity gradually decreases with further increases in defect contents $(\mathsf{X}\ =\ 150$ , 200). Therefore, the activity of $\mathsf{P t@U i O–66–N H_{2}.}\mathsf{X}$ presents an evident volcano-like trend, with the highest point at $\textsf{X}=\textsf{100}$ . Resorting to femtosecond timeresolved transient absorption spectroscopy, the relationship between charge separation efficiency and defect levels of UiO$66-N H_{2}-X$ has been studied to understand this volcano-type photocatalytic behavior. As far as we know, this is the first experimental demonstration on the different roles of MOF defects in photocatalysis, highlighting the importance of moderate structural defects.
The UiO-66-NH2-X with different contents of structural defects were prepared by the reaction of $Z r{\mathsf{C l}}_{4}$ and $N H_{2}$ -BDC (2-amino1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid) in N,N-dimethylformamide, with trace amounts of water and different equivalents of acetic acid (HOAc) as modulator, the working principle of which for defect formation was illustrated in detail (see Supporting Information, Section 6). All Ui $0.66\cdot N H_{2}-X$ $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}=\boldsymbol{0}$ , 50, 100, 150, 200) show similar powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns, suggesting their good crystallinity (Figure 1a). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) observation indicates that the MOF particles gradually change from small and intergrown prototypes to individual and octahedral nanocrystals, along with increasing particle sizes until $\mathsf{X}=100$ (Figure S1).
Several techniques have been adopted to investigate the degree of structural defects in UiO-66-NH2-X. First, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) curves of five UiO- $66-N H_{2}-X$ are qualitatively similar and present two well-resolved weight loss steps (Figure 1b). The weight loss in the range of $25-320\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ mainly corresponds with the loss of $H_{2}O$ adsorbate and HOAc modulator. As the temperature increases up to $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , the $N H_{2}.$
BDC linker fully combusts with complete collapse of the framework, as verified by the intense exothermic peak in the differential thermal analysis (DTA) curves. The final residue can be mainly assigned to the monoclinic $Z r O_{2}$ with some cubic $Z r O_{2}$ (Figure S2), which are normalized to $100\%$ for all samples to facilitate comparison (Figure 1b). Notably, the last weight loss step can be applied to compare the defect degree in different samples.[7c,d] The magnitude of weight loss in the last step corresponding to linker/framework combustion gradually decreases from UiO-66-NH2-0 to UiO-66-NH2-200 (Figure 1b), indicating the linker content in MOFs gradually reduces and more defects exist, exactly as expected. Therefore, it can be established that the modulator amount in synthetic process is inversely correlated to the amount of weight loss, while it is positively correlated to the defect degree in UiO- $66-N H_{2}-X$ . We then resorted to the dissolution/1H NMR technique, an effective tool to identify the ratios between $N H_{2}$ -BDC linker and HOAc modulator coordinated to Zr-oxo clusters (Figure 1c).[7d] The significant signal enhancement assignable to ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{C O O}}^{-}$ can be observed in the 1H NMR spectra of digested UiO- $66\cdot N H_{2}\cdot X$ with gradually increased X values, suggesting the incremental linker substitution by HOAc modulator. Intuitively, when the dicarboxylate linker is substituted by an end-capped monocarboxylate modulator, additional pore space would be created in the MOF, which provides higher surface area and affects the properties of U $\mathsf{J i O-66-N H_{2}}\mathrm{{\times}.}$ Unsurprisingly, ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake gradually increases for $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}-X}$ synthesized by introducing more HOAc modulators (Figure 1d). Consequently, Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area increases from 705 to $1057~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ and pore volume increases from 0.53 to 0.62 $\mathsf{c m}^{3}/\mathsf{g}$ , for Ui ${\mathsf{O}}{\mathsf{-}}66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2^{-}}{\mathsf{X}}$ with increased $\mathsf{x}$ and more defects (Table S1), while their pore size distributions do not present significant change (Figure S3). Notably, the influence of structural defects on the stability of ${\mathsf{U i O}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ has been demonstrated to be negligible (Figure S4-S6). Moreover, the formation of structural defects in UiO-66- $N H_{2}-\lambda$ X is believed to be the missing linkers, according to our reaction conditions and previous reports.[7e,7j,7k,9b]
The UV–vis spectra of all $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}-X}$ show similar curves in 250–800 nm (Figure S7–S11), and the calculated band gaps are all $\mathord{\sim}3.0\ \mathfrak{e V}$ (Table S2). The results indicate that the lightharvesting ability of these UiO- $66-N H_{2}-X$ with slight change in color (Figure S12) is hardly influenced by structural defects or particle size.[9a]
To boost the transfer and utilization of charge carriers in UiO-66- $N H_{2}-X$ for photocatalysis, $\sim1~\mathsf{w t\%}$ Pt NPs as co-catalyst were incorporated into the MOFs to afford $\mathsf{P t@U i O-66-N H_{2}-X}$ composites (Table S3), by impregnating UiO- $66-N H_{2}-X$ in the Pt precursor solution, followed by reduction at $200\ {^{\circ}}\mathsf{C}$ in an ${\sf H}_{2}/{\sf A}{\sf r}$ atmosphere.[10] Powder XRD patterns suggest that the introduction of Pt NPs does not affect the crystallinity of UiO-66- $N H_{2}-X$ (Figure S13). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) observation indicates that Pt NPs with an average size of $1.2\mathrm{-}$ $1.4\ \mathsf{n m}$ are dispersed evenly in $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{X}$ particles (Figure 2a, 2b, S14–S18). Then, we set out to investigate the photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production over $\mathsf{P t@U i O–66–N H_{2}.}\mathsf{X}$ with triethanolamine (TEOA) as sacrificial agent. The Pt@UiO-66- $N H_{2}-0$ , in which the MOF featuring few structural defects was synthesized without modulator, presents negligible ${\sf H}_{2}$ production $(9.4~\mathsf{\upmu m o l{\cdot}g^{-1}\cdot h^{-1}})$ . The very poor activity of $P\{\ @\mathsf{U i O}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathsf{N H}_{2}{\cdot}0$ may be attributed to non-overlapping between the empty $d$ orbitals of $Z r^{4+}$ and the $\pi^{\star}$ orbital of the linker and the obstruction of electron transfer from the linker to $Z\mathsf{r}^{4+}$ .[6f,9a] With the generation of more structural defects, $P t@{\mathsf{U i O}}{\mathsf{-}}66{\mathsf{-N}}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{-}}50$ and $P t@U i O-66\ –\mathsf{N H}_{2^{-}}100$ exhibit much enhanced ${\sf H}_{2}$ production rates of 154.2 and $381.2~\mathsf{\mu m o l{\cdot}g^{-1}}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively, suggesting that structural defects play a critical role in photocatalysis. However, the photocatalytic activity of $P t@U i O-66\ –\N H_{2^{-}}150$ and Pt@UiO$66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathrm{-}200$ , with further incremental structural defects, drops to 302.1 and $276.8\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. Therefore, the photocatalytic activity shows a volcano-type trend on the whole, and $P t@\mathsf{U i O}_{-66-\mathsf{N H}_{2}-100}$ presents the highest activity among all the investigated photocatalysts (Figure 2c, S19a). Moreover, $P t@U i O{-}66{-}N H_{2}{-}100$ is well recyclable for at least 10 cycles (Figure 2d, S19b), and its structure can be well retained, without any noticeable leaching or aggregation occurred to Pt NPs, after photocatalytic cycles (Figure S20, S21). The impressive volcano-type behavior explicitly manifests that, though suitable content of structural defects is favorable, too high concentration is detrimental to the activity. In addition, as a preliminary demonstration, the tandem reaction of photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production and nitrobenzene hydrogenation over Pt@UiO-66- $N H_{2}-100$ can be promoted, in the absence of additional hydrogen source (Figure S22).
To better understand the mechanism behind the observed volcano-type activity trend, we further resorted to femtosecond time-resolved transient absorption (fs-TA) spectroscopy to examine the involved charge separation processes.[11] In the fsTA measurements with a pump–probe configuration, the pump was set at $400\ \mathsf{n m}$ , a wavelength suitable for promoting electrons from the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ , whereas the probe was from a white-light-continuum source covering a spectral region of 500–700 nm. The representative fs-TA spectra recorded on $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}\mathsf{-}0$ and UiO-66-NH2-100 show that different levels of structural defects bring on no essential variation in spectral profiles (Figure 3a). However, pronounced changes can be detected in their kinetic behavior (Figure 3b). Considering that relaxation kinetics usually depends on probing wavelength, we conducted a global fit to retrieve the relaxation constants, in which a set of kinetic traces ranging from 580 to $650\ \mathsf{n m}$ with a 10-nm interval were used. The fitting results are as follows: $\mathsf{T}_{1}=81\pm2$ ps $(54\%)$ and $\bar{\mathsf{T}}_{2}=689\pm24$ ps $(46\%)$ for $\mathsf{J i O}{-}66{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2^{-}}0$ ; $\bar{\mathsf{T}}_{1}=67\pm2$ ps $(55\%)$ and $\mathsf{T}_{2}=617\pm23$ ps $(45\%)$ for UiO-66-NH2-50; $\mathsf{T}_{1}=20\pm1$ ps $(29\%)$ and $\mathsf{T}_{2}=384\pm$ 5 ps $(71\%)$ for UiO- $66\ –\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -100; $\mathsf{T}_{1}=174\pm5$ ps $(61\%)$ and $\bar{\mathsf{I}}_{2}=$ $1062\pm59$ ps $(39\%)$ for $J i O\mathbf{-}66\mathbf{-}N H_{2}$ -15; and ${\bar{\mathsf{T}}}_{1}=218\pm6$ ps $(70\%)$ and $\mathsf{T}_{2}=1492\pm113$ ps $(30\%)$ for UiO-66-NH2-200. The average relaxation lifetimes are $615\pm22$ , $553\pm21$ , $376\pm5$ , 881 $\pm53$ , and $1167\pm101$ ps for UiO ${\mathsf{I}}{\mathsf{-6}}6{\mathsf{-N}}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{-}}$ X $\mathrm{\Delta}X=0$ , 50, 100, 150, 200, respectively), as also annotated in Figure 3b. As compared in Figure 3c, the variation of such a set of average relaxation lifetimes presents an exactly inverse relation to the observed volcano-type trend of ${\sf H}_{2}$ production rates at different defect levels $\mathrm{\Delta}X=0{-}100$ ; 100–200). The average relaxation lifetime can be employed as an indicator to evaluate charge separation efficiency: the faster the relaxation, the higher the efficiency. The fastest relaxation observed in UiO-66-NH2-100 echoes well to the most predominant ${\sf H}_{2}$ production rate of $P t@\mathsf{U i O}{-}66{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2^{-}}100$ indicating that UiO-66-NH2-100 with a moderate defect level achieves the highest charge separation efficiency. It is worth noting that the average relaxation lifetimes of UiO-66- $N H_{2}.$ -X $\langle{\mathsf X}=$ 150, 200) are longer than those of UiO-66-NH2-X $(\mathsf{X}=0$ , 50), again indicating that the too high defect density in UiO-66-NH2-X $(\mathsf{X}\ =\ 150$ , 200) is not beneficial for promoting electron–hole separation efficiency.
Transient photocurrent measurement and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were conducted to further verify the volcanic activity of $\mathsf{P t@U i O–66–N H_{2}.}\mathsf{X}$ photocatalysts. As expected, the $P t@\mathsf{U i O}{-}66{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}{-}100$ presents the highest photocurrent response among all the samples (Figure 4a), implying that the creation of structural defects with optimized contents is vital to efficient separation of photogenerated electron–hole pairs, leading to switching on photocatalysis. This judgment is further supported by the EIS results (Figure 4b), where $P t@U i O-66\small{\cdot}N H_{2}\mathrm{-}100$ exhibits the smallest radius, manifesting the lowest charge-transfer resistance. Combined with the above results, it is assumed that, moderate defect levels may cause a decrease in energy of the unoccupied $d$ orbitals of Zr atoms and benefit the separation and transfer of photogenerated charge,[9a] while excessive structural defects, conversely, can turn into recombination centers of electron–hole pairs, thus causing reduced activity.[3e,12]
In summary, by selecting UiO-66-NH2 as a model, the specific influence of structural defects on photocatalysis has been systematically investigated. The photocatalytic ${\sf H}_{2}$ production presents an interesting volcano-type trend along with incremental contents of structural defects in the MOF. Impressively, $P t@U i O{\cdot}66{\cdot}N H_{2}{\cdot}100$ , with optimized content of structural defects, possesses the highest photocatalytic activity, highlighting that the creation of moderate structural defects is vital to promoting the efficient electron–hole separation. Remarkably, the significant distinction of charge separation efficiency caused by different defect levels has been systematically evidenced by fs-TA spectroscopy in terms of relaxation kinetics. Taken together, these results unambiguously demonstrate that the creation of structural defects can switch on photocatalysis and its content optimization is critical to photocatalytic performance. This work provides significant inspirations on the creation of structural defects in functional materials for optimizing performance, particularly in semiconductor(-like) materials for enhanced photocatalysis.
